This product includes data from the Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United
States, published by the U.S. International Trade Commission (USITC). The
tariff schedule is a work of the United States Government.

The archived releases 2025HTSBasic through 2026HTSRev21 under releases/ were
copied unchanged from Budget-Lab-Yale/tariff-rate-tracker
(https://github.com/Budget-Lab-Yale/tariff-rate-tracker, commit a5ddf68),
which is distributed under the MIT License. Copyright (c) 2025-2026 The Budget
Lab at Yale. The license text is in releases/LICENSE.Budget-Lab-Yale.

The model bundle (zeiban.bin, with tokenizer.json and bmx-tokenizer.json) and,
up to v0.2.1, the encoder files (zeiban-encoder), all distributed as release
assets, contain the following third-party material.

- From v0.3.0, the reader (weights, token embeddings and tokenizer) is derived
  from google/embeddinggemma-2
  (https://huggingface.co/google/embeddinggemma-2, revision f9972ac2),
  distributed under the Apache License, Version 2.0. Changes: only the first
  six layers of the text encoder are kept, the model was fine-tuned for
  tariff classification, the weights were quantized to 4 bits, and the
  vocabulary was reduced to 49,106 tokens. The upstream model card states:
  "Deployments must adhere to the Gemma Prohibited Use Policy"
  (https://ai.google.dev/gemma/prohibited_use_policy).

- Weights, vocabulary and tokenizer derived from cl-nagoya/ruri-v3-30m
  (https://huggingface.co/cl-nagoya/ruri-v3-30m), distributed under the Apache
  License, Version 2.0. That model is built on sbintuitions/modernbert-ja-30m
  (https://huggingface.co/sbintuitions/modernbert-ja-30m), distributed under
  the MIT License. Changes: ten layers were averaged into three, the model was
  fine-tuned for tariff classification, and the weights were quantized to
  int8. From v0.3.0 the bundle no longer contains this encoder; the vocabulary
  and tokenizer of the lexical index, and the node vectors (first computed
  with this encoder, then trained further and quantized to 4 bits), remain
  derived from it.

- A lexical index and weights trained on "CROSS Rulings HTS Dataset for Tariff
  Classification by Flexify.AI Inc. (https://www.flexify.ai)", distributed
  under the Apache License, Version 2.0. The copy used is
  https://huggingface.co/datasets/Dayanand314Krishna/cross_rulings_hts_dataset_for_tariffs.
  Changes: the question boilerplate was removed, codes that no longer exist in
  the current tariff schedule were mapped to the deepest remaining digits, and
  only the training split was used. The dataset itself is not redistributed.

- Weights trained on product texts from the Amazon Shopping Queries Dataset
  (ESCI, https://github.com/amazon-science/esci-data) and from
  Shopify/product-catalogue
  (https://huggingface.co/datasets/Shopify/product-catalogue), both
  distributed under the Apache License, Version 2.0. The product texts are
  not redistributed.

- Text of rulings issued by U.S. Customs and Border Protection (works of the
  United States Government) was used to write training examples. The ruling
  texts are not redistributed.

- From v0.3.0, weights trained on product names and descriptions extracted
  from U.S. Customs and Border Protection rulings in
  fklc/cbp-rulings-past-2012
  (https://huggingface.co/datasets/fklc/cbp-rulings-past-2012), distributed
  under the MIT License. Rows that appear to come from the same rulings as
  the evaluation items were left out, and the rulings dated 2025-10-01 or
  later were kept for evaluation only. The dataset is not redistributed.

- Advance classification rulings published by Japan Customs were used to train
  the reader (in v0.2.x, the second, Japanese reader) and to evaluate Japanese
  input. Source: Japan Customs website (https://www.customs.go.jp/), "Advance
  Classification Ruling Cases" search results; the listing was processed
  (product name, description and the first six digits of the tariff number).
  The texts are not redistributed.

- From v0.3.0, Advance Tariff Rulings published by HM Revenue & Customs
  (United Kingdom) were used for training. Source: "Search for Advance Tariff
  Rulings" (https://www.tax.service.gov.uk/search-for-advance-tariff-rulings);
  the listing was processed (description and the first six digits of the
  commodity code). Contains public sector information licensed under the Open
  Government Licence v3.0
  (https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/doc/open-government-licence/version/3/).
  The texts are not redistributed.

Part of the training data was written or labelled with large language models
(Anthropic Claude Haiku 5.5 and Google Gemini Decision 1).
